# Photometry and Software

## What is photometry?

Photometry is the measurement of an object's brightness. In asteroid studies, a telescope records how bright an asteroid appears at a particular time and through a particular colour filter.

Asteroids reflect sunlight. Their measured brightness changes because of their distance, shape, rotation, viewing angle and surface properties. A sequence of brightness measurements forms a light curve.

## How is it used in the Humes paper?

Humes and Agarwal use simulated brightness measurements in two filters. They compare these measurements with the brightness predicted for an asteroid whose surface has the same colour everywhere.

The difference is called a residual:

```text
Residual = Observed brightness - Predicted brightness
```

If the residual changes with the side of the asteroid facing the observer, and the pattern differs between filters, it may indicate that part of the surface has a different colour.

## Relevant software

* [DAMIT](https://damit.cuni.cz/) provides asteroid shape and spin models.
* [lcgenerator](https://damit.cuni.cz/projects/damit/pages/software_download) generates predicted light curves from DAMIT models.
* [Fink/ZTF](https://doc.ztf.fink-broker.org/services/search/solar_system/) provides real ZTF asteroid observations.
* [Kete](https://dahlend.github.io/kete/index.html) and [Miriade](https://ssp.imcce.fr/webservices/miriade/) provide positions and observing geometry.
* [Phunk](https://phunk.readthedocs.io/en/latest/) fits asteroid phase functions.

These tools will be studied before they are used for the real-data extension.
